{"word": "insecure", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "insecure", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪnsəˈkjʊə(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪnsəˈkjɔː(ɹ)/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪnsəˈkjʊɹ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪnsəˈkjɝ/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪnsəˈkjɔɹ/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "more insecure", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "most insecure", "tags": ["superlative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Not secure.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "It was, as Carrados explained, entirely his own preposterous fault. Nothing but curiosity about the size of the room had impelled him to touch the walls, and the picture, having a weak cord or an insecure nail ... had it not brought something else down in its fall?", "reference": "1927, Ernest Bramah, Max Carrados Mysteries:"}, {"text": "The Yung-li ruler, finding Chao-ch’ing insecure, fled to Wu-chou (梧州), Kuangsi province, and then to Kuei-lin, vigorously pursued by the Manchu army.", "reference": "1982, Albert Chan, “After the Fall of Peking”, in The Glory and Fall of the Ming Dynasty, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, →OL, page 371:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "несигурен"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "опасен"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "insegur"}, {"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "不安全的"}, {"language": {"code": "da", "name": "Danish"}, "word": "usikker"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "onbeveiligd"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "epävarma"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "epäluo